@page "/sourcery/no-i-am-not-afraid-a-wizard-will-take-my-job"
@using Sorcery.ServerSide.Components.Blogging;
@using Sorcery.ServerSide.Components.Footnotes;
@using Sorcery.Blogging;
@inject BlogBook BlogBook;

@code {
    public static readonly RenderFragment Introduction = __builder =>
    {
        <Paragraph>
            They say this new Wizard is great and will replace all programmers.
            My days are numbered and I better seek a different career because soon I will be obsolete.
            Maybe there are some issues with the Wizard and he's not quite as good yet, but it is only a matter of time.
            We're in the Early Days™ they say.
            It's 2025. It's 2022. It's 2014. It's 2006. It's the same old story every time and magic still doesn't exist.
        </Paragraph>
    };
}

<BlogPost Post="BlogBook.NoIAmNotAfraidAWizardWillTakeMyJob">
    @Introduction
    
    <Paragraph>
        I am not a person that likes labels, but there is one that I can't deny &ndash; I am a programmer.
        As of this writing I'm 27 and I've been programming for 11 years, 8 in a "they pay me for this" kind of way 
        <Footnote>
            Most people call that <q>professional capacity</q> but if I was a professional at 18
            then what meaning could that word possibly have.
        </Footnote>.
        Programming brings me joy. Since you're reading this, there's a chance you know what I'm talking about.
        There's this feeling you get from writing a neat little program, running it, seeing it do what you wanted it to do.
        From playing a simple game, but one you wrote yourself. From writing a simple script that will sometimes
        replace a random word on a webpage with <q>ass</q> and installing it on your teacher's PC.
        I guess you can call it <q>satisfaction</q>, but it feels like it lacks profundity. It lacks whimsy.
        The actual feeling has a lot more <q>oompf</q> to it, a taste that words just don't quite do justice.
        So I am a programmer and I will remain one,
        no matter if I'm employed in industry, in academia, unemployed, or retired and playing with whatever new hardware
        we'll have in 2065 if humanity will still be able to make hardware by then.
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph>
        I am also extremely lucky: One, I have found something that I'm passionate about; two, it can be
        converted into reasonable amounts of money; three, I had education access that allowed me to get good
        at it. It's <em>a lot</em> of luck. Since high-school I was dead sure I was going to be programming for a living.
        And the world's been trying to convince everyone and their mother that it's a doomed career path since forever.
    </Paragraph>
    
    <Header2>End of Eldorado for IT</Header2>
    <Paragraph>
        When I was younger I was mostly exposed to Polish news and punditry. There is this one particular phrase
        that will live rent-free in my head until the day I die &ndash; <q>End of eldorado for IT</q>. Now, El Dorado
        is a non-existent city that Spanish colonists used to tell myths about, filled with gold and riches, and
        only non-white people to guard it. In this context it's used as an allegory for untapped wealth that requires
        little effort to access, essentially a gold rush. I don't know why Polish news love that particular word so much,
        or why they spell it as <q>eldorado</q>.
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph>
        The first article with that headline I read was published in 2014. Since then, a similar one appeared
        <em>every&mdash;single&mdash;year.</em> Multiple times a year. It's a phrase so popular Polish Google autocompletes
        it for you when you type in <q>End of e</q>. The message has always been the same &ndash; it's currently very
        easy for young people to get a job in IT that pays well, but surely the end is just around the corner because
        of X. That X will either be mysterious market forces, interest rates, sun spots, whatever you can imagine.
        In 2014 it was, believe it or not, blockchain.
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph>
        Ah yes, blockchain, 2014 was the year Ethereum was (ill)-conceived.
        I can't find that damn article now since searching for anything on the Internet is pure pain nowadays, but its main
        thrust was that there would be no more programmers doing normal stuff, everything would be blockchain-based; thus,
        anyone getting an education that wasn't focusing on blockchain would be unhirable and would end up flipping patties
        at McDonald's. Assuming these weren't moved on-chain as well, I guess. Now remember that we're talking about
        articles written by such renowned knowers-of-technology like Forbes, so it's of course no surprise they had
        absolutely zero idea what they were talking about.
    </Paragraph>

</BlogPost>
